ested in the  the ethical content here the nature of  human happiness she says that this is a  city in which the citizens or residents  are actually quite happy she says joyous  how is one to tell about joy how to  describe the citizens of Omaha  she says they're not simple people  although they were happy and she thinks  that considering these matters is  important for us in our late modern  times because we've actually got some  mixed up ideas about happiness she says  that we tend to make certain assumptions  so one of the first things that she  tells us has to do with the you might  say political organization of this city  she says there was no king they did not  use swords or keep slaves I do not know  the rules and laws of their society but  I suspect they were singularly few so  the norms governing them are relatively  manageable you could say as they did she  says without monarchy and slavery so  they also got on without the stock  exchange the advertisement the secret  police and the bomb so it sounds like  these are people who are living a  simpler life and and this would actually  make good sense given the fact that you  know Ursula K Le Guin has noted at many  different points that the political  ideology or theory that she comes  closest to is that of anarchism  and in this case it's not anarchism  though there actually is a structure to  this society she tells us that I'm going  to jump ahead to technology they they  might not have cars or helicopters in  and above the streets and she says this  follows from the  that the people of Omaha are a happy  people but she tells us and here's one  of the early lessons about the nature of  happiness happiness these are her words  is based on a just discrimination of  what is necessary what is neither  necessary nor destructive and what is  destructive and she says in this middle  category that of what is neither  necessary nor destructive that of  comfort luxury exuberance they could  perfectly well have things